Motivated by some of the reviewer’s insightful comments and questions regarding the regret behavior in the large-$\bar{p}$ and small-$K$ regime, we are pleased to share that our analysis can be naturally extended to yield even stronger results with only minor modifications.

Specifically, we can show that the regret does **not** increase with $K$ at all even in the regime of the large-$\bar{p}$ and small-$K$ setting, i.e., when $K\cdot\bar{p}^{K-1} > 1$. By leveraging standard techniques from prior works [2, 3], we establish that our algorithm (without any modification) also satisfies the instance-independent bound $\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(d\sqrt{T})$. As a result, we obtain the following refined instance-dependent upper bound $\mathcal{R}(T)=\tilde{\mathcal O}\big(\min\lbrace K\bar{p}^{K-1},1\rbrace \cdot d\sqrt T\big)$, along with a matching lower bound of  $\Omega \big(\min\lbrace K\underline{p}^{K-1},1\rbrace \cdot d\sqrt{T}\big)$ up to $\bar{p}$ and logarithmic factors. 

Please note that these additional results are obtained through a minor refinement of our existing proof techniques. We provide a brief sketch of the argument below and will include a formal corollary and full proof in the final version of the paper.

**1. Supplementary Regret Upper Bound of UCB-CLB**

**Step 1**: Introduce a worst-curvature parameter $\tilde{\theta}\_{t,i}$. 

We adopt the intermediary parameter $\tilde{\theta}\_{t,i}$ inspired by Lee et al. [1] as follows:

$$
\tilde{\theta}\_{t,i} 
:= 
{\arg\min}\_{
    \theta 
    \in 
    \cup\_{\tiny \substack{\tau \in [t, T] \\ k \in [K]}
    } \mathcal{C}\_{\tau, k}^{\texttt{ON}}(\delta)} \dot{\sigma}\_1(x_{t,i}^\top \theta), 
\quad \forall t \in [T], i \in \mathcal{I}
$$

This ensures that the gram matrix $H\_t$ satisfied the following lower bound:
$$
\begin{align}
H\_t 
\succeq 
\sum\_{\tau=1}^{t-1} 
\sum\_{i \in O\_\tau}
\dot{\sigma}\_1(x\_{\tau, i}^\top \tilde{\theta}\_{\tau, i})
x\_{\tau,i}x\_{\tau,i}^\top + \lambda I\_d =: L\_t. 
\end{align}
\tag{1}
$$

**Step 2**: Control the deviation of the derivative.

Combine Lemma E.3 of Lee et al. [1] with our concentration (Corollary 5.5), we get:
$$
\begin{align}
\dot{\sigma}\_1(x\_{t,i}^\top \theta^\star)
\le
\dot{\sigma}\_1(x\_{t,i}^\top \tilde{\theta}\_{t,i}) 
+
\frac{\beta\_{T,K}(\delta)}{4}
\Vert x\_{t,i} \Vert\_{H\_t^{-1}}.
\end{align}
\tag{2}
$$

**Step 3**: Upper bound on the prediction error via the curvature-weighted norm.

Starting from the 2-nd Order Taylor Expansion, and applying Equation (1) and Equation (2), we obtain the following:
$$
\begin{align}
\sigma\_1(u\_{t,i}) - \sigma\_1(x\_{t,i}^\top \theta^\star)
\le
2\beta\_T(\delta)\dot{\sigma}\_1(x\_{t,i}^\top \tilde{\theta}\_{t,i}) \Vert x\_{t,i} \Vert\_{L\_t^{-1}} + \frac{7 \beta\_{T,K}^2(\delta)}{10} \Vert x\_{t,i} \Vert\_{H\_t^{-1}}^2.
\end{align}
\tag{3}
$$

To simplify notation, we define $\zeta\_{t,i} := 2 \beta\_T(\delta) \dot{\sigma}\_1 (x\_{t,i}^\top \tilde{\theta}\_{t,i}) \Vert x\_{t,i} \Vert\_{L\_t^{-1}}$, which corresponds to the first term on the right-hand side of the inequality.

**Step 4**: Applying Equation (3) in the Regret Decomposition.

Following the regret decompostion of our Theorem 5.3 and substituting Equation (3) for the bound on $\sigma\_1(u\_{t,i})-\sigma\_1(x\_{t,i}^\top \theta^\star)$ leads immediately to the following inequality for the regret $\mathcal{R}(T)$:

$$
\begin{align}
\mathcal{R}(T)
\le
\underbrace{
\mathbb{E}
\left[
    \sum\_{t=1}^{T}
    \sum\_{i\_{tk} \in C\_t}
    \prod\_{l=1}^{k-1}
        \sigma\_0(x\_{t,i\_{tl}}^\top \theta^\star) 
    \zeta\_{t, i\_{tk}}
    \prod\_{m=k+1}^{K}
        \sigma\_0(x\_{t,i\_{tm}}^\top \theta^\star)
\right]
}\_{\text{term 1}}
+
\text{2$^{\text{nd}}$ Order Term}
\end{align}
$$

**Step 5**: $K$-free Control of Term 1 
Directly leveraging the proof technique of Lemma 19 in Liu et al. [2], we easily get the bound of term 1 as follows:

$$
\begin{align}
\text{term 1}
\le
\sqrt{
T
\cdot
\mathbb{E} 
\left[ 
    \sum\_{t=1}^{T} 
    \sum\_{i\_{tk} \in C\_t}
    \frac
    {\prod\_{l=1}^{k-1} \sigma\_0(x\_{t,i\_{tl}}^\top \theta^\star) \zeta\_{t, i\_{tk}}^2}
    {\sigma\_0(x\_{t,i\_{tk}}^\top \theta^\star)\sigma\_1(x\_{t,i\_{tk}}^\top \theta^\star)}
\right]
}
\end{align}.
$$
Then, substituting the definition of $\zeta\_{t,i}$ and using the fact that $\dot{\sigma}\_1(x\_{t,i}^\top \tilde{\theta}\_{t,i}) \le \dot{\sigma}\_1(x\_{t,i}^\top \theta^\star)$ holds with high probability due to the concentration result in Corollary 5.5 yield the followings:
$$
\text{term 1}
\le
2
\beta\_{T}(\delta)
\sqrt{
T
\cdot
\mathbb{E} 
\left[ 
    \sum\_{t=1}^{T} 
    \sum\_{i\_{tk} \in C\_t}
    \prod\_{l=1}^{k-1} \sigma_0(x\_{t,i\_{tl}}^\top \theta^\star)
    \dot{\sigma}\_1(x\_{t,i\_{tk}}^\top \tilde{\theta}\_{t, i\_{tk}})
    \Vert x\_{t, i\_{t,k}} \Vert\_{L\_t^{-1}}^2
\right]
}
$$

